1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Perhaps it is a little too early to talk about the Advance Variation of the French Defence. But believe me when I say the Advanced Variation of the French Defence is the most challenging way to play against. Let's examine the position.-White gets a spacious advantage-White can launch a potential attack on the kingside since black usually doesn't have that much defender around the kingside. -White can cause complications on the kingside with early Queen moves.-If White was able to hold unto his central pawn black would certainly crumble!-Last but not least, Black's one of biggest problem, the LSB. Certainly White does get a lot of PROS in here. But that does not mean white gets an easy play in here! Black would attack white's central control right away. Since white's center is loose and not fortified white often does not hold on to his spacious advantage. White is free to relinquish his spacious advantage. But that only comes if black does not do anything.Black's major breakthrough in here is the f pawn and the c pawn. The e pawn is often the remain of White's advanced pawn formation. } { [%csl Yb4,Gd4,Yf6,Ge5,Yc5][%cal Re8g8,Rb8c6,Yc7c5,Rg8e7,Rf8b4,Rf7f6] } 3... c5 { The best response against the Advance Variation. Black needs to fight back against White's central pawn or otherwise suffocate! } 4. c3 { As usual there are couple of alternatives in here such as Nc3. But c3 is the most favoured move in here. The move c3 supports the center. } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 (5. Bb5 { Bb5 is often common amongst Club Players. I do believe that Bb5 is not White's strongest move in here since it doesn't bare any challenges to black. Its a waste of move and white is often obligated to take the knight for the bishop or otherwise retreat back the bishop and lose a tempo. } 5... Qa5 6. Qa4 (6. a4 cxd4 7. Qxd4 Qxb5 8. axb5 Nxd4 9. cxd4 Bd7 { Black is eventually doing better in here! Black has the initiative since be has the bishop pair. }) (6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. Nf3 Ne7 9. O-O Ba6 10. Re1 Nf5 11. Nbd2 Be7 12. Nb3 Qb6 13. Bf4 c5 14. g4!? { Perhaps a bit dubious in here. Why is white willing to play g4 thus weakening his kingside? } 14... Nh4 15. Nxh4 g5 (15... Bxh4? { Bxh4 is just not possible because of h5! The bishop is now trapped! } 16. g5)) 6... Qxa4 7. Bxa4 Ne7 { The exchange of queens would do we for black. Black is headed for an endgame in here. } 8. dxc5 Bd7 9. f4 Nxe5 10. Bxd7+ Nxd7) 5... Bd7 { The Light Squared Bishop is a bad bishop in here since it is blockaded by Black's light square pawn chain. An usual idea in here is to manuever the bishop to White's kingside from e1 to h5. } { [%csl Yd7,Yh5,Ye8][%cal Ye8h5,Yd7e8] } 6. a3 c4 { c4 is possible once the move b3 was eventually played. Keep in mind that the advance of pawns to c4 is not possible if White's a or b pawn is still complete. } 7. Be2 Nge7 8. O-O Na5 { Nb3 is the plan in here, Black's knight is ready to put his knight towards the b3 square. White's knight is stuck defending the b3 square. } 9. Nbd2 Qc7 10. Re1 h6 11. Nf1 Nb3 { Nb3 was made possible since White's knight moved away. The only piece defending the b3 square. } 12. Rb1 Ba4 13. Bf4 g5 14. Bg3 Nf5 15. Ne3 O-O-O 16. Nxf5 exf5 17. h3 f4 18. Bh2 Kb8 19. Nd2 Nc5 20. Qc1 Ne6 *


