1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 { The French Defence on the board! It would only take a couple of times before you master the French Defence! } 3. e5 { The Advanced French Defence, the most challenging and the best response for white if white wants to withhold tensions. } 3... c5 { Eventually, there's really not much of a good option other than the move c5 immediately striking back towards white's expansion towards the center. } 4. Nc3 { Developing the knight, eventually since the Winawer French could no longer be achieved in here since the c file is already closed. Black has to make ways in getting the light square bishop off. } (4. c3 { Another alternative in which eventually supports White's overexpansion towards the central pawns. } 4... Nc6 5. f4!? { f4 is only beneficial once black has castled kingside. Eventually white is aiming for a Dutch Defence or Bird Opening idea where an immediate attack on the kingside can be made once black castles. Black really has nothing much to worry about in here other than castling. } (5. Nf3 { Nf3 is the main line in here. Nf3 simply developing a piece and further progress can be made out of piece development and activity in here. } 5... Qb6 { Qb6 eyeing on the g2 pawn and at the same time support the c5 pawn breakthrough. } 6. Be2 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nge7 8. Na3 Nf5 { Keep in mind that the move Nf5 is often useful in the French Defence. Always keep in mind that if Nf5 is available depending on the situation. Place your knight on the f5 square! } 9. Nc2 Be7 10. O-O Bd7 11. Bd3 Rc8 12. Bd2 O-O { End of Opening Preparation. I do believe that both opposing sides are doing well. Black has already castled and developed all his pieces. further progress can be made by a minority attack on the queenside. }) 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Qb6 7. Bd3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bd7 (8... Nxd4?? { Nxd4 loses a knight! Always remember that taking back with the queen once Black's knight is captured is met with a light square bishop check! Thus losing a queen! } 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Bb5+) 9. Bc2 Nb4 { It is advisable to get rid of the bishop pair once you have the chance. Well the position is frankly on the verge of opening up. Once white castles the move f3 is dangerous thus White's overexpanded central control is long bygone! } 10. Nc3 Nxc2+ 11. Qxc2 Bb4) (4. Bb5+ { Bb5 check Is frankly not a bad move. But total equality is what both opposing side should expect. The exchange of bishops would simplify the position and is much easier to play. Since white has played Bb5 white is obligated to either take the light square bishop thus Black's problem with the bad bishop is gone. Or retreat back the bishop in which loses a tempo. } 4... Bd7 5. Bxd7+ Nxd7 6. c3 (6. Be3 cxd4 7. Bxd4 Qg5 { Black eventually has the edge in here. White is underdeveloped thereby rapid development is on the board onset for black. } 8. Nf3 Qxg2 9. Rg1 Qh3 10. Nc3 a6) 6... Ne7 7. Nf3 Nc6 { Notice how Black's pieces are placed on the queenside. Black's play is often on the queenside rather than the kingside. It is advisable not to castle right away since castling right away would only lead to a horrible position since an attack on Black's kingside is very dangerous and problematic. } 8. O-O Be7 9. Be3 Qb6 10. Qc2 O-O) (4. Bd3 { White is just looking forward for a nice game of chess and total equality is made in here. Eventually white did not aim for variations in which gives black much challenges other than the move Bd3. } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 Nxd4 6. Nxd4 cxd4 7. Nd2 Ne7 8. O-O Nc6 9. Nf3 Be7 10. Re1 Bd7 11. a3 O-O) 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 (5. Bb5? { I do believe that the move Bb5 is a mistake here, in the French Defence Advance Variation the Light Square Bishop is useful since white's pawn is placed on the dark square. Swapping off bishops would only weaken the light squares. But considering that Bb5 is played it is frankly a waste of move since white has to exchange bishop for knight or otherwise retreat back the bishop thus losing a tempo. } 5... cxd4 6. Qxd4 Bd7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6) 5... cxd4 6. Nb5 { Black may have the chance to keep this pawn in the near future but in exchange white has a strong counterplay since the d6 square is entitled for White's knight. } 6... Bc5 7. a3 { White is planning to play b4! With a minority attack on the queenside and at the same time the d6 square is available for White's knight to take advantage of. } (7. Nfxd4? { Not possible since the d pawn us going to be taken away by Black's knight. } 7... Bxd4 8. Nxd4 Nxe5) (7. Nbxd4? { Not possible since the d pawn would be taken by black's knight. } 7... Bxd4 8. Nxd4 Nxe5) 7... Nge7 8. b4 Bb6 9. Nd6+ Kf8 10. Bd3 Ng6 11. Bg5 f6 12. exf6 (12. Nxc8 Rxc8 13. exf6 gxf6 14. Bh6+ Ke7 15. O-O Qd7) 12... Qxd6 *


