1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 { The French Defence on the board! One of the oldest and most played defence against white! } 3. e5 { The Advanced Variation of the French Defence. This is the most challenging French Defence variation ever. Black's goal is to breakup White's center whereas white would try and fortify the center. } 3... c5 { Black's key point is the move c5. Energetic breakthrough. Black will then have to deal with his bad bishop by swapping it of after the move a6. } 4. c3 { One of White's finest attempt and strongest reply in here. Trying to keep up with the overextended pawns. } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Bd3 (6. Be2 f6 { As a general rule, f6 can only be played once black has castled and his pieces are consolidated. But in here f6 is possible since White's passive response with his piece coordination could not challenge Black's choice of move. } 7. O-O fxe5 { Bishop to e8 to f7 is one of Black's idea with the bad light square bishop since this would eventually free up the bad bishop. } 8. dxe5 { The exchange of pawns would badly weaken White's d pawn. } 8... Qc7 9. Bf4 Nh6 10. c4 (10. Bxh6? { White has this illusion in which he believes that taking the knight with the bishop would endanger Black's king. The thing is black has the bishop pair and regardless whether the kingside pawn structure has been damaged. Black's king is still safe and more file has been opened to attack White's king. So if anyone is in danger in here it's white. } 10... gxh6 11. Re1 Bg7 12. Nbd2 O-O) 10... d4 { The move d4 is eventually very important in here. Black wants to attack White's kingside. Black should not exchange pawns opening up the d file for white. } 11. Nbd2 Be7 12. Ne1 O-O-O) (6. g3 { Eventually white wants to fianchetto his light square bishop since it is blockaded with the knight. I do believe that this move is respectable. } 6... Nge7 7. Bg2 Nf5 8. dxc5 (8. O-O cxd4 9. cxd4 Qb6 10. Be3 Qxb2) 8... Bxc5 9. b4 Bb6 10. O-O Qc7) 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Qb6 { Once the c pawn breakthrough has been achieved. The f6 breakthrough is what black should be aiming for later on. } 8. Bc2 Nb4 9. O-O Nxc2 10. Qxc2 Rc8 11. Nc3 Ne7 *


[Event "French Defence - Complete Repertoire: Chapter #4: The Advance Variation - Part #4"]
[Date "2022.07.11"]
[White "Chapter 3:"]
[Black "Advance Variation"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Fatty"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C02"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Euwe Variation"]
[StudyName "French Defence - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Chapter #4: The Advance Variation - Part #4"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cpULfWpl/smd8CxJg"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 { In the advance variation of the French Defense, White counters the threat to the e4 pawn by advancing the pawn, creating a space advantage with locked pawn chains in the center. The main line of the Advance Variation continues 3... c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 and then we have a branching point: 5... Qb6, the idea is to increase the pressure on d4 and eventually undermine the white centre. The queen also attacks the b2-square, so White's dark-square bishop cannot easily defend the d4-pawn without losing the b2-pawn. White's most common replies are 6.a3, 6.Be2 and 6.Bd3. } 3. e5 { As usual white should advance his pawn forward gaining a spacious advantaage, but in exchange white should be wary and be on the lookout of what he is entering, for things are about to be messy in this particular game. } 3... c5 (3... f6 { This is my favorite one, black immediately challenges white's less fortrified center right away with the move f3?! It is not advicable to play the move f3 if black hasn't castled or developed his pieces. Black creates an immediate weakness on the kingside and that is the light squares. Black's king is very vulnerable! But seriously why do I still like the move f3?! Well as a matter of fact since white hasn't developed any of his pieces yet theres no immediate attack on the kingside. The light square weaknesses can easily be patched with the move g6. Black will keep attacking white's center whereas white has to defend. Black also dreams of an open f file once black castles. Once the f file is open and the dark square bishop lands on c5 black has a very good attack against white. Problem in here is that white can constantly attack black as of then since black isn't really doing that much which postpones or discourages white's constant attack. } 4. Bd3 { White would usually target the weaknesses on black's kingside. For instance its the light squares around in black's king. Black needs to patch that weakness with the move g6! } 4... g6 5. h4 { Black would be in constantly defending in here. White's accountable assault on the kingside would have been a very difficult and challenging attack on black. Yet again it is not advisable to play the move f3 right away but sure it does work against players who are not well prepared, or at least against someone on a blitz or bullet time format. } 5... fxe5 (5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Nf7 7. Qe2 Nc6 8. c3 Bd7 9. b4 Bg7 10. Bf4 a6 11. a4 O-O) 6. dxe5 Bg7 7. Bg5 Ne7 { Here is one of the engine line in here. Stockfish formally describes this as the refutational line against the accelerated Napoleon Attack. Yet again I do not know how many times do I have to stress over this. f3?! Is not advisable, Im just warning you the consequences of playing the Accelerated Napoleon Attack. } 8. h5 Bxe5 9. Nc3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Qd6 11. f4 { Thus black's position is in great risk and danger in here. It would really take that long before black's position crumbles like France in World War Two. White has an uncountable assault on black's king. Thus making it hard for black to make a stubborn and awestruck resistance. } 11... Rf8 12. Nf3 Nbc6 13. hxg6 hxg6 14. Bb5 Bd7 15. Bxc6 Bxc6 { White needed to eliminate one of the knight in exchange for bishop due to black's ultimate weakness: the knight on e7. By eliminating one of the knights white can then play Rh7 thus forcing black to play Rf7 which would be very unpleasant since the king would have been stuck in danger. Keep in mind that taking with the b pawn would only damage and weaken black's position since Rb1 would have dominated black from the kingside and on the queenside. } 16. Rh7 Rf7 17. Rxf7 Kxf7 18. Qd4 Rg8 19. Qf6+ Ke8 20. Ne5 Rf8 21. Qg7 Qa3 22. Kd1 Rf5 23. Rb1 Qc5 24. Kc1 Rxe5 25. Qxe5 Qd6 26. Qg7 Kd7 { Although black's position is managable and defendable for the most part. Theres no doubt that white has the advantage here. White has successfuly tied black's king in a place where all his pieces are deprived of freedom to roam around the board, making plans and decision "Which is the best move to cause major challenges to my opponent that would push me further to winning the game?" }) 4. c3 { The usual response in this particular opening is the move c3 in which stabilises white's central pawns and also opens up the d1-a4 diagonal. } { [%csl Yc3,Gc5,Rd1,Yd4,Gd5,Ye5,Ge6,Re8][%cal Rd1a4,Ra4e8,Yc3e5,Gf8c5] } (4. dxc5 { "Why don't you wake up your your knight, ." "White intends Bc1-e3." "What about Nb8-c6, ? first of all the e5 pawn is very weak and the c pawn is also hanging." I frankly do believe that this is a mistake for white. White basically just wasted a tempo building up his center only to give it all away in the near future. } 4... Nc6 { Now white has two choices in here, either save the e pawn or save the c pawn. Saving the e pawn is much more preferable but lets take a look at other alternatives in which are inferior. } (4... Bxc5 { Bxc5 is eventually another good alternative but I prefer Nc6 since it targets both e and c pawn. Once black was able to capture the e pawn black's center would be stronger and the initiative is passed on black's. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Nc3 Qc7 7. Bf4 Nge7 8. Bd3 f5 9. Na4 b6 10. Nxc5 bxc5 11. O-O O-O) 5. Nf3 (5. Be3 { "Aren't you going to move your knight?" "I recommend that you should try Ng8-e7, Mr ." } 5... Nxe5 6. Nf3 Nxf3+ 7. Qxf3 Be7 8. Bd4 Bf6 9. Qc3 Bd7 10. Nd2 Qc7 11. Nf3 Bxd4 12. Qxd4 f6 13. c4 e5 14. Qxd5 { "Now tell me , what can the white king do?" "White intends 0-0-0." "If I was in your position I would go for Ng8-e7 in a millisecond followed by long castle 0-0-0." } 14... O-O-O) 5... Bxc5 6. Nc3 a6 7. Bd3 Nge7 { Black's plan is to castle, develop and improve his pieces then throw a minority attack on the queenside. Black could also try and target the overextended e pawn. } 8. O-O O-O) (4. Nf3?! { Whats the point of playing the Advance French Defence if you are going to let go of your central pawns easily and right away? White must be oblivious to do so! } 4... cxd4 5. Nxd4 (5. Qxd4 Nc6 6. Qg4 { [%csl Rf8,Rg4,Yg7][%cal Rf8c5,Rg4g7] } 6... Qc7 { Black is now aiming to attack the e pawn thus white has to response by playing Ng3 in order to protect the e pawn. I do believe that white is frankly doing bad in here since white's central e pawn is in a lot of pressure. } { [%csl Rc6,Rc7,Ye5][%cal Rc7e5,Rc6e5] } 7. Qg3 Nge7 8. Bd3 Ng6 9. O-O (9. Bf4?! { White's effort and attempt to save the e pawn would end up disastrous and a straight up fail because after knight takes bishop, queen takes bishop f6! White's queen is pinned! } 9... Nxf4 10. Qxf4 f6 { [%csl Rf4,Gf6][%cal Yc7f4,Gc6e5] } 11. Nc3 (11. Qg3 { Black is just winning the exchange here. } 11... Nxe5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5+ 13. Qxe5 fxe5) 11... Nxe5 12. O-O a6 13. Rae1 Bd7 14. Nxe5 O-O-O 15. Qe3 fxe5 16. Qxe5 Bd6) 9... Ngxe5 10. Nxe5 Qxe5 11. f4 Qc7 { Black is just winning the exchange here. Black is up a pawn and should be enough to secure advantage in here. } 12. Be3 g6 13. Na3 a6 14. c3 Bg7 15. Qf2 O-O) 5... Ne7 6. Bd3 Nbc6 7. Nxc6 Nxc6 8. Qe2 Be7 9. O-O O-O) (4. c4 { "You should really play Nc6, " "The silicon chessplayer would appear to be threatening Ng1-f3" } 4... Nc6 5. dxc5 Nxe5 6. Be3 Ne7 7. Nc3 Nf5 8. cxd5 Nxe3 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. fxe3 Bxc5 12. Qe2 O-O) 4... Nc6 { Nc6 is the usual main line in here. Although other alternative such as Bd7 is frankly possible. But I prefer Nc6 first followed by Bd7 Ne7 and so on. } (4... Bd7 { Black's idea in here is to play Qb6 followed by Bb5 to exchange the bad light square bishop to white's good light square bishop. } 5. Nf3 Qb6 { [%csl Rf7,Rd5,Re6,Rd7][%cal Rf7d5] } (5... a6 6. Bd3 Bb5 (6... Nc6) 7. Bxb5+ axb5 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. b4 Ba7 10. Qe2 Nd7 11. Na3 Ne7 12. Nxb5 Bb8 13. Bg5 O-O) 6. Be2 Bb5 7. O-O Bxe2 8. Qxe2 cxd4 9. cxd4 Ne7 10. Nc3 Nec6 11. Be3 Nd7 12. Rac1 Be7) (4... f6 { f3 was made possible in here because white played the move c3 thus the move Bd3 is delayed. Unlike the move f3?! right away after e5! Things would have gotten a bit cranky after the move Bd3. c5 followed by f6 is better since black challenges white's center right away with the move c5 thus forcing white to react right away with the move c3 (Other alternatives are not as strong as the move c3) Since black challenges both white's center on the queenside and the kingside. White would not be able to attack right away since black has some tactical sequences which would be able to counterattack white's attack. } 5. Bd3 Qc7 6. Nf3 fxe5 (6... Nc6 7. O-O fxe5 8. dxe5 Nh6 9. Bxh6 gxh6 10. c4 d4 11. Nbd2 Bd7 12. Ne1 Qxe5) 7. dxe5 (7. Nxe5 { "You must cover the square h5, " White's goal is to give a check at h5 thus g6 is forced and winning the exchange after Nxg6. } 7... Nf6 { [%csl Rd1,Re8][%cal Rd1h5,Rh5e8] } 8. Bg5 Nbd7 9. Bxf6 Nxf6 10. Bb5+ Kd8 11. O-O Bd6 12. Re1 Rf8 13. Nd2 cxd4 14. cxd4 Qb6) 7... Nc6 8. O-O Nh6 9. c4 d4 10. Qe2 Nf7 { Black exerts some effort in trying to win the e pawn. White's goal is to keep his e pawn afloat or otherwise suffer the inevitable. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf7e5,Rc6e5] } 11. Bf4 Be7 12. Nbd2 g5 13. Bg3 Bd7 14. Rae1 a5) (4... a6!? { This has fallen out of favour and only seen a few times. The idea in here is to play Bd7 followed by pawn takes pawn then Bb5! Exchanging the light square bishop to white's! However many do not like this because first of all the b pawn is doubled and the queenside pawn structure is damaged. Black do get some compensation due to the fact that black has a semi opened a file. } 5. Bd3 Bd7 6. Nf3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bb5 8. Bxb5+ axb5 9. O-O Nc6 10. Nc3 b4 11. Ne2 Nge7 12. Ng3 { "I would have thought that moving your knight to g6 would be an excellent idea." "Your opponent is planning Qd1-e2." "Black should most certainly consider Ne7-g6" } 12... g6 13. Bg5 Bg7 14. Qd2 h6 15. Be3 Nc8 16. Rfc1 O-O) (4... Qb6 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. a3 Nge7 7. Bd3 Bd7 8. dxc5 Qxc5 9. O-O Ng6 10. b4 Qb6 11. Re1 Rc8) (4... b6 { A fun sideline in which black plays Ba6 to exchange his bad light square bishop into white's strong and good light square bishop. Although this is a bit dubious due to the fact that black isn't aiming to break up white's center in blitz. It is still a fun sideline to surprise your opponent. } 5. Nf3 Qd7 6. Bd3 Ba6 7. Bxa6 Nxa6 8. O-O Ne7 9. Na3 Rc8 10. h4 Nc6 11. Nc2 Be7 12. Bf4 cxd4 13. cxd4) 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Be2 Nge7 { Black should play Nf5 after followed by Qb6 and so on. Just a quick tip, do not play Qb6 not until the dark square bishop is not supporting the c pawn since the queen would just be harrassed if the black queen were to do the dark square bishop's duty. } { [%cal Re7f5,Rf8e7,Rd8b6] } 7. O-O Nf5 8. Na3 cxd4 9. cxd4 Be7 10. Nc2 O-O 11. Bd3 f6! { Quick tip, black should not play f6, not until black has eventually castled. (There are exceptions if its really an emergency) } 12. Bf4 Rc8 13. Bxf5 exf5 14. Re1 Be8 15. exf6 Bxf6 *


