1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { The classical French Defence. Usually the most common opening variation against the French Defence since this is very flexible and easy to play. Black may face some certain challenges when it comes to this position. But eventually the Classical is less challenging than the Advance French Defence. } 3... Bb4 { This is the Winawer Variation of the French Defence Classical Variation. There are plenty of alternative in here including the move Nc6 but I do believe that the French Winawer is the best variation. First of all you are threatening to exchange bishop for knight thus ruining the queenside pawn structure. Second of all black prevents white from making further progress until he resolves his conflict with the pin of the dark square bishop. } 4. e5 { Advancing the pawn forward is the best option white has in here. Other moves is less challenging. We would focus on the move e6 in here.Since white has eventually push his e pawn forward up to the fifth rank black would face certain problems when it comes to space. White has the spacious advantage and the only way black could resolve that is to break up White's center with the c pawn and f pawn outbreak.Now about the dark square bishop, black may find certain challenges since black would usually be obligated to exchange bishop for knight thus weakening the dark squares. Black must be aware of his position's need and safety before further progress can be made towards the central pawn breakthrough. } 4... c5 5. a3 { A3 is White's strongest move since this forces black to make a hasty position of whether exchanging bishop for knight would be ideal of not. } 5... Bxc3+ { Exchanging the bishop for knight is the best move in here since this ruins White's kingside structure and the c pawn could be advance forward to blockade White's light square bishop. But everything comes with a cause! Now the g file is open for White's rook to take advantage of! } 6. bxc3 Qc7 (6... c4? 7. Qg4! { Black is straight up busted in this particular position since white would aim for ack's weak kingside right away. There's no piece protecting the b2 pawn and the king is the only available piece able to defend it. } 7... Kf8 8. h4) 7. Qg4 { White is already threatening to take the b pawn. White can practically take the b pawn in the near future but black has set a certain tactical trap in which would cause white the whole game. } 7... Ne7! { Black is willing to sacrifice pieces in here in exchange black has a tactic on White's vulnerable king in here. } 8. Qxg7 (8. Bd2 { Bd2 is the best move in here preventing any initiative or tactical sequence of move for black in here. Well since this move is much safer for white then Black can castle safely. } 8... O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bh6 Ng6 11. Bxg6 fxg6 12. Be3 Qf7 13. Ne2 Nc6 14. O-O Ne7) 8... Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 10. Ne2 Nbc6 { Black Is once again setting up a plot in here. Black has laid so much trap in this opening variation that every move for white is a landmine! Well on figurative speaking. } 11. Rb1 dxc3 12. Bf4 Bd7 13. g3 O-O-O { Black has castled queenside and despite of the open g file in which may be dangerous somewhat. White's king is vulnerable since white cannot castle kingside since the kingside pawn has been removed thus opening more file for Black's rook to maximise on. } 14. Bg2 Ng6 *


