1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 { One of white's strongest move is the move a3. The move a3 tries to get rid of the bishop pin of course. Well position is about to be complicated for both opposing sides since black is obligated to take the knight with the bishop followed by the move pawn takes e4. } (4. Ne2 { This is the Alekhine Gambit, black temporarily snatches a pawn but in exchange white has laid a bunch of traps for black. I suggest keeping things simple and just play on! } 4... dxe4 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Nxc3 Nc6 7. Bf4 Nf6 8. Bb5 Nd5 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Bd2 Qf6) 4... Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 dxe4 6. Qg4 { Eventually the move Qg4 is a hasty choice for white since black can easily defend after the move Qd6. But other alternative includes Nf3! A better pawn sacrifice! } 6... Nf6 (6... Qf6 { Because of white's weak and horrible pawn structure on the queenside. Black is ready to sacrifice a pawn and quickly get into an endgame where black is on offense. } 7. Qxe4 Qg6 8. Qxg6 hxg6) 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 Bd7 { Two pieces were developing right away but in exchange black would lose one pawn. } 9. Ne2 c5 10. Rb1 Bc6 11. Bg5 Rg6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Qe3 Nd7 14. dxc5 Qg5 { Apparently White's horrible queenside structure followed by the lack of development makes it easy for black to win back those pawns. } *


