1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bd2 { In this particular chapter, we will uncover the sidelines of the French Defence Winawer Variation. This is the Fingerslip variation of the Winawer French. White wants to prevent a double pawn on the queenside and a3 would be the next idea in here. } (4. Bd3 dxe4 { You do not have to necessarily complicate things! Take the pawn and life would be a lot easier than it used to be! } 5. Bxe4 Nf6 6. Bf3 c5 7. Ne2 Nc6 8. O-O O-O 9. a3 Bxc3 10. bxc3 cxd4 11. cxd4 Bd7) (4. Qd3 Nf6 5. e5 { As expected, white would eventually play this uneasy move thus brining us back to the Advance French Defence. } 5... Ne4 6. Ne2 c5 7. a3 c4 8. Qh3 Qa5 9. g4 Nxc3 10. bxc3 Be7) 4... dxe4 5. Qg4 { Qg4 is the typical idea at the Winawer French Defence. This is a very complicated matter since black is ready to sacrifice a pawn but in exchange black gets the initiative and active development. } (5. Nge2 Nf6 6. Nxe4 Bxd2+ 7. Nxd2 O-O) 5... Nf6 6. Qxg7 Rg8 7. Qh6 Qxd4 8. Nge2 Qe5 9. Bf4 Qf5 10. h3 Bd6 *


