1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. c4!? { By playing 4.c4, White gains some space at the expense of not being able to defend the d-pawn. Thus this variation isn't really suitable for white since black is the one who most benefits from the move c4?! } 4... Bb4+ { Bb4+ turns the position into a Bogo Indian Against white's usual Queen's Gambit. } 5. Nc3 (5. Bd2 { Another variation in which white exchanges bishops for bishops leading to a less complicated position. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 Nf6 7. Nf3 Be6 8. cxd5 Qxd5 9. Nc3 Qd6 10. Bb5+ Nbd7 11. O-O O-O-O { Thanks to white's isolated pawn due to this c4 move, the d pawn remains a target and is weak because it is isolated. }) 5... Nf6 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Bd3 (7. g3 Re8+ 8. Be3 Ng4) 7... Bg4 8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nc6 10. Be3 Bh5 11. a3 Bd6 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 Be7 14. d5 Ne5 15. Be2 Ng6 16. Bg3 Bd6 { 1-0 White wins. } *


