1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 { This is the King's Indian Attack of the French Defence.White's goal is primarily simple. First and formost overturn the position into an inverted d4 set up. White wants to employ the King's Indian system. Now, white wants to lure black from extending his pawn forwards and it would have been bloody difficult to get things done. Black has to play a safer system against this. So let me introduce you an opening variation in which hasn't been quite seen often in the French Kings Indian. } 3. Qe2 { Qe2 is quite Unorthodox and hasn't been seen for quite some time. This move has grown out of favor since white usually wants to play Nf3 g3 Bg2 as often seen in the King's Indian. } 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 (4. f4 { White wants to expand his control on the center. Quite a logical choice, setting up a Bird Opening system can also be applied well for white. } 4... Bc5 5. Nf3 dxe4 6. dxe4 Nf6 7. Bd2 (7. h3 { Thia does prevent the knight jumping to g2. The only problem is this allows Nh5 then g3! The dark squares around the kingside is heavily weakened! } 7... Nh5 8. Qc4 Qe7 9. Rh2 Ng3 10. c3 { a6 followed by b5 is a good idea in here. White's position is heavily cramped. Feel the domination of Black's French Defence! } 10... a6 11. Nbd2 b5 12. Qd3 Bb7 13. Ng5 Nxf1 14. Kxf1 Rd8) 7... O-O 8. Nc3 Ng4 { Nf2 is a very strong offensive attack against white's kingside. Although position is roughly defendable for white, white would have a hard time dealing with this. } 9. h3 Bf2+ 10. Kd1 Ne3+ 11. Kc1 Nxf1 12. Rxf1 Bc5) 4... e5 { Simply take the center if you can but don't overextend! Otherwise you would overpress and thereby lose the game! } 5. g3 Bg4 6. c3 { Black needs to protect the d pawn with the f pawn with f6! } 6... f6 7. Bg2 Qd7 8. Nbd2 { Black should long castle! } (8. h3 { h3 a good alternative to kick off the bishop. Black has to retreat to preserve the bishop pair. } 8... Be6 9. O-O O-O-O 10. exd5 Bxd5 11. Rd1 Be6) 8... O-O-O 9. O-O *


