1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 { Nd2 is the usual main line in here. White is planning to play Nf3 g3 Bg2 short castle 0-0 and so on. Trying to overturn the position into an inverted d4 set up. } 3... Nc6 4. Ngf3 e5 { Theres no better way to address this, if the center is open take it! But don't overextend otherwise overpress! } 5. g3 { g3 white switches into his as usual set up of the King's indian. Black can then aim from the center pawns. } (5. exd5 Qxd5 { Another sidenote is exchanging pawns which leads to simplification. } 6. Be2 Nge7 7. O-O Ng6 8. c3 Be7 9. Nc4 O-O 10. b4 Rd8) (5. Be2 { Be2 would have been less provocative in here. But as always better to be safe than sorry. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O Bd6 7. c3 O-O) 5... Bg4 (5... Be6 6. Bg2 d4 { Black gains a comfortable spacious advantage in here. But not to mention that this could also come up with potential risk since white's goal would have been to attack the center. } 7. O-O f6 8. Qe2 Bc5 9. c3 Qd7 10. Nb3 Bb6 11. cxd4 exd4 { Better to let go of the center and get on with life! Better output better choices! } 12. Bd2 Nge7 13. Rfc1 O-O) 6. c3 { Makes way for the queen, this idea is usually seen in the Czech Defence. } { [%csl Ra4][%cal Rd1a4] } (6. h3 { h3 kicks the bishop but in exchange white weakens his kingside. } 6... Be6 7. Bg2 f6 8. O-O d4 9. Nb3 Qd7 10. c3 O-O-O) 6... Nf6 7. Qa4 Qd7 8. Bg2 O-O-O { Black has a strong attack on the kingside. } { [%cal Yh6h4,Yh7h5] } *


