1. e4 e6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 { The Two Knights AttackThis is my favorite variation in the Caro Kann. I wasn't aware that this is also useful against the usual French Defence. White's goal is simply to wait and see what black has in his sleeves. Either advancing the pawn leading to sophisticated game. Or simply exchange pawns and move on with life. } 3... c6 { This is also possible with the Caro Kann. Black waits for white's response before taking any immediate actions. } 4. d4 Bb4 5. e5 (5. Bg5 { Worth mentioning I guess but a superior variation for black. White wastes a move for no particular reason aside from harassing the queen. } 5... f6 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 dxe4 8. Nd2 f5 9. Qh5+ g6 10. Qe2 Nf6 11. f3 exf3 12. Nxf3 Ne4) (5. Bd3 { Another sideline in which white simply develops his pieces and have a good game. } 5... dxe4 6. Bxe4 Nf6 7. Bd3 c5 8. O-O cxd4 9. Nxd4 O-O) 5... Ne7 6. Bd3 c5 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qa5 9. Bd2 c4 10. Be2 h6 *


