1. e4 e6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 { The Two Knights Variation attempts black to pushing the pawn forward thus creating a weak centralized pawns. Funny enough I tried pushing the pawn in the Caro Kann which did transpose into this line. I overpress and I had to give up my center. White got a comfortable play whereas I was cramped for the rest of playable game. } 3... d4!? { Although d4 is recommended by top engines such as Stockfish and Dragon. d4 is still a complicated line. Black has to thoroughly study how to deal with his weak centralized pawns. One mistake and the center would collapse thus white's position would begin to shine. } 4. Ne2 c5 { A series of opening traps would arise from this position alone. b4!? Alone is complicated enough! } 5. c3 { As usual white would have to try and break up the center before he suffocates into an "Old Benoni System" c3 is the way to go! } (5. b4 a6!? { b4!? Enough makes this unfeasible for majority such as me. Black is gambiting a pawn but in exchange white's position is a few moves away from collapsing. Reminds me of the Holy Roman Empire or the British Empire. It was overextended and poof! } 6. Bb2 cxb4 7. Nexd4 Nf6 { Black's best hope in here is to simply give back the pawn and then develop on piece. I don't think anyone likes being challenged into a chaotic and stressful position. Unless you are a madman! } 8. e5 Nfd7 9. h4 Qc7 10. c3 Nxe5 11. cxb4 Bxb4 12. Rc1 Nbc6 13. Nxc6 Nxc6 14. Bxg7 Rg8 15. Bf6 Rg6 16. Bg5 Qd6) (5. Ng3 { Another alternative in which white didn't go for a stronger pawn break without the knight on e2. } 5... a6 6. Bc4 h5 7. h3 b5 8. Be2 Nf6 9. a4 h4 10. Nf1 Qa5) 5... Nf6 6. e5 (6. cxd4 cxd4 { The exchange of pawns would only lead to simplification } 7. Qa4+ (7. Nexd4 Nxe4 8. b3 Bd6 9. Bb5+ Nd7 { Keep things simple would you? } 10. O-O O-O) 7... Bd7 8. Qxd4 Nc6 9. Qe3 Nb4) 6... Nfd7 7. cxd4 cxd4 8. Nexd4 Nxe5 9. Bb5+ Nec6 10. Nxc6 Nxc6 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 *


