1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 { The move Nd2 prevents Black from entering the Winawer (3.Nc3 Bb4). The knight on d2 exerts no pressure on d5 and may lead to slower development of white's pieces but the move is solid and leads to games of a more positional nature than its main alternative of 3.Nc3. In here however I wouldn't recommend playing Nc6!? e5! Would have been a long struggle for black. Instead just exchange pawns and keep things simple. The Rubinstein variation is similar to the Karpov Variation of the Caro Kann and it also doesn't really deal with chaotic and complications in the opening itself. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 { The Rubinstein Variation is a simpler way to handle the Tarrasch Variation. First and formost it boycotts theoretical "homework" In a sense where you have to study a bunch of lines and move orders such as Nc6?! Instead of pawn takes pawn in the opening. Second white doesn't have that much pressure on the center. White would have to either retreat back his knight on the kingside or exchange Knights leading to equalization. } 6. Nxf6+ { There are other more theoretical lines than knight takes knight such as the knight retreating back to g3. But all the matter are covered and discussed in the latter parts of this opening study. } (6. Bg5 { A sideline in which deals with pinning the bishop and have me mumbling words discussing the matter of this line. Just simply defend with the bishop and you're off to go! } 6... Be7 7. Ng3 h6 8. Bf4 c5 { Keep in mind that this breakthrough is actually mandatory if you wanna strive for victory. } 9. Be2 Qb6 10. O-O cxd4 (10... O-O) 11. Rb1 Nd5 12. Bc1 Bc5) (6. Ng3 { Knight retreats to g3 in an attempt to complicate and persuade such elicit victory. } 6... c5 7. Bd3 Be7 8. O-O O-O) 6... Nxf6 7. c3 Be7 8. Bd3 c5 { This breakthrough is actually mandatory if you wanna strive for victory. } 9. Bf4 cxd4 10. Nxd4 O-O *


