1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 { Another variation against the Tarrasch Variation. Black strikes the center attempting to break white's central advantage. With the help of white's knight on the second rank Nd2 White would have to spend a tempo ahead just to make way for his dark square bishop. Black should attempt to break up white's central pawns. } { [%csl Yc6,Ye4,Yd4,Ye7,Yf5,Rc5,Rd5,Ya6,Yd7][%cal Yc8d7,Re4d5,Yf8e7,Ya7a6,Yg8e7,Rd4c5,Yb8c6,Rc2c3,Rg1f3,Ye7f5] } 4. c3 { White is going to advance his e pawn soon after black's turn. Black has to exchange his d pawn now or otherwise it would have been too late! } (4. exd5 exd5 { White exchanging his central pawns would just be beneficial for black. Black has now achieved a powerful central pawn formation. } { [%csl Rc5,Rd5][%cal Re8g8,Rf8e7,Rg8f6] } 5. Ngf3 Nf6 6. c3 Be7 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. O-O O-O) (4. dxc5 Bxc5 { Exchanging pawns would only benefit black. White gives up one of his central pawn and a swift development is ahead of black's game. } 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Ngf3 Nf6 7. O-O O-O) (4. Ngf3 Nf6 { Nf6 transposes into a theoretical Advance French Defence. But to keep you covered just aim for your standard French Defence set up or make a little modification if necessary to fight white. } 5. e5 Nfd7 6. c3 Qb6 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. O-O cxd4 { It is necessary to temporarily sacrifice a pawn for white. White would be able to win back the pawn in the near future. } 9. cxd4 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Nf3 Qb6 12. Qa4 Be7 { White's next intention is to move the queen to g4 thus targeting black's kingside. } 13. Qg4 g6 14. Bh6 Qb4 15. Qh3 { [%csl Rd7][%cal Rd7c5] } 15... Nc5 16. Rad1 { [%csl Yc8][%cal Yc8d7] } 16... Bd7 17. Ng5 Qxb2) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 { Simply develop your pieces. Play Be7 Nf6 short castle 0-0 and you would be doing roughly fine afterwards. } { [%csl Ye7,Yf6,Yg8][%cal Yf8e7,Ye8g8,Yg8f6] } 5... cxd4 6. cxd4 Be7 7. Bd3 Nf6 8. Nf3 O-O *


