1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { The Classical Variation This is the move that is often played in the French Defence.White develops his knight and keeps the tension on the center. Black has many options in here. One of them is Nf6!? And Bb4! All of which are highly theoretical. Although the Winawer variation and the usual Nf6!? Are covered in this French Defence in this course. On a brief meantime let me introduce you to my number one recommendation in the Classical French. } 3... dxe4 { Pawn takes pawn! dxe4 is roughly discouraged amongst the purist. Why is that? Well first and formost black disrupts white's central pawn but in exchange white gets a comfortable and flexibly play, thus causing havoc amongst black's position. Black is forced to play Nd7 followed by Ngf6. All of which makes the light square bishop stuck and uncanny, plus black's position is temporarily dislodged.However this idea is also played in the Caro Kann with Nd7 the Karpov Variation. e6 and c6 is forced but doesn't necessarily makes black's position completely cramped. Black's light square bishop can be fianchettoed via b6 and Bb7. The knight on d7 can be rerouted to f8 to g6. The knight on f6 can also be rerouted from Nf6-d5 } 4. Nxe4 Nd7 { This is the Rubinstein Variation of the Classical French Defence. Although this isn't quite as popular comparatively against competitive Steinitz and Winawer variation. However this is my preferable and favorite variation to meet against the Classical French. Pawn takes pawn might look loke black is actually giving up the center. Simply not true! He would play c5 and the battle for the center Begins! } 5. Bd3 (5. c3 { The Kasparov Attack Kasparov played this often c3 against the Rubinstein French Defence. The intention here is simple. Protect the d pawn to avoid any tactics and to make way for the queen from d1 to a4 } 5... Ngf6 6. Bg5 { Other variation include Ng3 but bg5 is also often played. You know, just to keep you all covered I guess! } (6. Ng3 Be7 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 { Find this important breakthrough in which challenges and suppresses white's central forces. } 8... c5! { An important breakthrough to dislodge white's advantageous central pawn. } 9. O-O { Your light square bishop is stuck! Any way to bring it upfront on the game? } { [%csl Yc8,Yb7] } 9... b6 10. Bf4 Bb7 11. Qe2 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rf6d5] } 11... Nd5 12. Bd2 { [%csl Ya8][%cal Ya8c8,Yd8c7] } 12... Rc8 13. Rad1 Qc7 14. Rfe1 { Simply take white's d pawn thus forcing white either to take with the c pawn thus causing an isolated weak pawn or take with the knight which is a better move. } { [%csl Yc5][%cal Yc5d4] } 14... cxd4 15. Nxd4 N7f6) 6... Be7 7. Nxf6+ { [%csl Re7][%cal Re7f6] } 7... Bxf6 8. Bxf6 Nxf6 9. Nf3 O-O) (5. g3 { Perhaps rather a bit "Unorthodox" But a good variation to meet the Rubinstein just so the light square bishop would be dislodged and challenged from being fianchettoed to b7 } 5... Ngf6 6. Bg5 { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rf8e7] } 6... Be7 7. Nxf6+ Bxf6 8. Bxf6 Nxf6 9. Bg2 { [%csl Ye8,Rc7][%cal Ye8g8,Rc7c5] } 9... O-O 10. Nf3 c5 11. dxc5 { How do you win back material? Particularly the c pawn? } 11... Qa5+ 12. c3 Qxc5 13. O-O { [%csl Yc8][%cal Yc8d7] } 13... Bd7) 5... Ngf6 { Simply inviting white to exchange knight. This is the main reason why Nd7 is played just so Ngf6 would be mate possible without damaging the kingside pawn structure. } 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. Nf3 c5 8. Be3 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Bd8c7] } (8. c3 { Simply protecting the d pawn. Keep in mind that cxd4 after pawn takes pawn would lead to an isolated pawn. } 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 { Black needs to castle fast! What must black do to compensate for the tempo? } { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bf8b4] } 9... Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 O-O) 8... Qc7 { Simply slide your queen to c7 to avoid any nasty tactical sequences of moves. } 9. O-O Bd6 10. Qe2 O-O *


