1. e4 e6 2. f4!? { A playable variation against the French Defence. But the problem in here is that this weakens the dark square diagonal on black's kingside with h7-g1. This also allows Ng6-Nf5. White cannot capture since the bishop is blocked by the f pawn. Furthermore this also slightly weakens white's kingside aswell. } { [%csl Rg1,Rb6][%cal Rb6g1,Rb6a7] } 2... d5 3. e5 c5 { Simply occupy the center since white is keeping it ignored. White would then play c3 followed by d4! To feud off black's central expansion. } 4. c3 (4. Nf3 Nc6 5. c3 Nh6 { Black's knight would find a great outpost from. h6-g5! } 6. d4 (6. Na3 Qb6 7. Be2 { Simply play Be7 then castle short 0-0 followed by knight development. } { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rf8e7] } 7... Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Nc2 Bd7 10. d4 cxd4 11. cxd4 f6 { f6 is advisable once black has eventually castle kingside. Although f6 is still possible if necessary against other move order. Keep on mind that this often comes with risk so beware. })) 4... Nh6 5. d4 Nc6 6. Nf3 { Develop your queen to b6! } { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rd8b6] } 6... Qb6 7. dxc5 { Take with the bishop followed by short castle 0-0 in the near future. Throw a minority attack on the queenside if necessary and you would most likely be fine with life! } 7... Bxc5 8. Bd3 Bd7 9. Qe2 a5 *


