1. e4 e6 2. c4!? { c4 the Steiner Variation weakens the d pawn. Because of white's weakening move of e4 and c4 the d pawn is effectively an isolated pawn. } 2... d5 { White will take either with the e or the c pawn. Both of which doesn't practically really matter as long as black knows what he is doing. } 3. exd5 (3. cxd5 exd5 4. exd5 Nf6 { Taking with the queen would just lose tempo after Nc3. Play Nf6 instead! You will win back the pawn either ways. } 5. Nf3 Nxd5 6. Nc3 Bf5 7. d4 Bb4 8. Bd2 O-O) 3... exd5 4. cxd5 (4. d4 { Transposes into an Exchange French Defence. As you may notice white expands his central control at the expense of an isolated d pawn. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 Bb4+ 6. Nc3 O-O { [%csl Rf8][%cal Rf8e8] } 7. Bd3 Re8+ 8. Be3 dxc4 { Simply take the pawn so that the d pawn would remain as a weakness. } 9. Bxc4 Qe7 10. O-O Be6) 4... Nf6 { Taking with the queen would just lose tempo after Nc3. Simply play Nf6 and the position looks similar to a Panov Attack Caro Kann. } 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. d4 Bb4 7. Bd2 O-O *


